The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (!tdev->rx_channel && (tdev->rx_mmio != tdev->tx_mmio))
	tdev->rx_channel = tdev->tx_channel;
...
if (tdev->rx_channel)
	mbox_free_channel(tdev->rx_channel);
```
Reason: When `rx_channel` is not obtained from the device tree but the RX MMIO exists (different from TX MMIO), the code aliases `rx_channel` to point to the same channel as `tx_channel`. The subsequent unconditional free on `rx_channel` then calls `mbox_free_channel` on the same channel object that was already freed (or will be freed) via `tx_channel`, causing a double-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (tdev->rx_channel && tdev->rx_channel != tdev->tx_channel)
	mbox_free_channel(tdev->rx_channel);
```
Reason: The added check ensures that `mbox_free_channel` is only called on `rx_channel` when it is a distinct channel from `tx_channel`. This prevents the double-free when `rx_channel` is aliased to `tx_channel`. The same fix is applied in both the `err_free_chans` label of `mbox_test_probe` and in `mbox_test_remove`.

